  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 122 AM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nWednesday...The upper ridge center will be across north Texas with \nupper ridging into the Intermountain West. An upper trough will \nextend across the Great Lakes. This will place western Nebraska in \nnorthwest flow aloft. Surface low pressure is forecast to be across \nsoutheast Colorado. A disturbance will move across southern South \nDakota and Nebraska, bringing likely POPs to north central Nebraska \nand portions of southwest and central Nebraska. NBM 12hr \nprobabilities for greater than 0.25 inch is from 30 to 60 percent \nacross portions of north central into central NE during the day.\n \nWednesday night into Thursday morning...a disturbance in the western \nNE panhandle will move eastward Wednesday night. With a fairly moist \natmosphere and an increasing H85 low level jet, this will bring \nanother good opportunity for scattered to widespread wetting \nrainfall. Likely to categorical POPs for Wednesday night. NBM 12hr \nhour probabilities for greater than 0.25 inches is from 50 to 65 \npercent across the Sandhills between Valentine and North Platte into \ncentral Nebraska. The GEFS, ECMWF and Canadian ensembles all support \nwetting rain across this same general area. \n\nAnother period of chance POPs Thursday afternoon into Thursday night \nas a disturbance in west-northwesst flow aloft moves across the \nregion.   The upper ridge is forecast to build eastward into the \nregion Friday through Sunday. Mainly dry conditions, although \nisolated thunderstorms are possible.\n\nHighs Wednesday and Thursday from the upper 70s to low 80s north \ncentral to the upper 80s to near 90 southwest. Friday should see \nhighs close to normal in the upper 80s to low 90s west, with heat \nreturning to the 90s Saturday and upper 90s to near 100 Sunday.",
